# DURABLE REPRO — a struct holding a `Mutex[T]` FIELD alongside ANY other
# droppable field aborts (exit 134, `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`)
# on the SELF-HOST lane only. Rust gg (both backends) prints and exits 0.
#
# RED-VERIFIED 2026-07-29 at HEAD (branch `worktree-agent-aa503e0e5c6ae0e04`):
#   Rust gg (C backend)         -> stdout `hello-heap-forced`, exit 0
#   Rust gg (LLVM backend)      -> stdout `hello-heap-forced`, exit 0
#   Self-host (driver --emit-c) -> stderr `free(): double free detected in
#                                  tcache 2`, exit 134 (SIGABRT)
#
# SCOPE — this fixture samples exactly ONE cell of the discovered axis:
# `Mutex[int]` paired with a `String` field in an ordinary struct, moved into
# the struct with `!`. The following adjacent cells were probed by the scout
# and CONFIRM the axis is "Mutex field + ANY droppable companion field",
# INDEPENDENT of order:
#   - Mutex[int] alone (no sibling field)          -> SH exit 0 (works)
#   - Mutex[int] + int (trivial)                   -> SH exit 0 (works)
#   - Mutex[int] + String  (this fixture)          -> SH exit 134
#   - Mutex[int] + Vector[int]                     -> SH exit 134
#   - String first, Mutex[int] second              -> SH exit 134
# The discriminator is the presence of a SECOND droppable field, not the
# String vs Vector inner type and not the field order. Not probed: RWLock /
# Channel / Shared / Weak companions (a sibling family per Core #12).
#
# The bug reproduces WITHOUT any `.lock()` call — it is purely a drop-path
# defect in the self-host, so no exercising of the Mutex is required.
#
# MECHANISM (hypothesised, NOT chased to the writer site — do not treat as
# established): the self-host struct-drop path double-frees the Mutex handle
# when the containing struct has ANOTHER field that owes its own drop, most
# likely a per-field drop emission mis-order or a drop-flag double-emit that
# only Mutex's runtime `gorget_mutex_free` (single-owner, unique-free) trips
# because a second free abort()s in `tcache`. The Rust lane's own Mutex-drop
# machinery (Core #8 Inc-A, `82de2110`) does not exhibit this on the same
# shape. Distinct from every filed Guard-family bug: no Guard, no `.lock()`,
# no field-access through a guard here.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): printing the String field prints
# `hello-heap-forced` on ALL lanes and the process exits 0 cleanly. The String
# is heap-forced (concat of two runtime String parts, per Core #12 payload
# axis) so no const-fold can elide the droppable-companion axis.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE CANNOT RUN GREEN on the self-host until fixed — the SH build
# succeeds and the emitted binary aborts. It is `#[ignore]`d and asserts the
# INTENDED Rust-lane behaviour.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (UNOWNED HIGH -> Concurrency subsystem SH-lag).

struct Holder:
    Mutex[int] m
    String tag

void main():
    Mutex[int] mu = Mutex[int](42)
    Holder h = Holder(!mu, "hello" + "-heap-forced")
    print(h.tag)
